WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.199 --> 00:00:02.839
Now that we've got a workable character,

2
00:00:02.839 --> 00:00:04.759
we should probably deal with animation.

3
00:00:04.759 --> 00:00:06.839
We're all familiar with the concept of a skeleton

4
00:00:06.839 --> 00:00:07.879
in a games engine.

5
00:00:07.879 --> 00:00:10.599
It's a series of bones in a hierarchical framework

6
00:00:10.599 --> 00:00:13.529
with joints and restraints applied.

7
00:00:13.529 --> 00:00:15.589
However, in Unreal, the skeleton asset

8
00:00:15.589 --> 00:00:18.190
is also responsible for associating animation data,

9
00:00:18.190 --> 00:00:20.510
not just providing the skeletal hierarchy

10
00:00:20.510 --> 00:00:22.719
within the skeletal mesh.

11
00:00:22.719 --> 00:00:24.199
A skeleton asset can be shared

12
00:00:24.199 --> 00:00:26.000
between other skeletal meshes

13
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:27.039
and these skeletal meshes

14
00:00:27.039 --> 00:00:30.030
don't necessarily have to match exactly.

15
00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:31.510
Sharing of a skeleton asset

16
00:00:31.510 --> 00:00:34.030
provides a way to share animation between characters

17
00:00:34.030 --> 00:00:37.570
or even entire animation blueprints.

18
00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:40.679
The skeleton asset is responsible for the following.

19
00:00:40.679 --> 00:00:42.159
Animation retargeting,

20
00:00:42.159 --> 00:00:44.079
and this allows animations to be reused

21
00:00:44.079 --> 00:00:46.759
between characters that use the same skeleton asset

22
00:00:46.759 --> 00:00:49.250
but may have different proportions.

23
00:00:49.250 --> 00:00:52.070
It's responsible for creating and storing sockets

24
00:00:52.070 --> 00:00:54.070
and sockets allow developers to place a point

25
00:00:54.070 --> 00:00:56.390
within the skeleton that is offset from a bone.

26
00:00:56.390 --> 00:00:57.670
And this is specifically useful

27
00:00:57.670 --> 00:01:01.479
for attachment of weapons to a character, for example.

28
00:01:01.479 --> 00:01:04.040
Next, it can store animation notifies

29
00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:07.459
and these are gonna be discussed in depth in just a second.

30
00:01:07.459 --> 00:01:09.459
They can store animation curves

31
00:01:09.459 --> 00:01:10.540
and these curves can be used

32
00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:13.829
to drive material parameters, for instance.

33
00:01:13.829 --> 00:01:16.349
Lastly, they can store animation slot names

34
00:01:16.349 --> 00:01:18.269
and these are useful as these can be used

35
00:01:18.269 --> 00:01:22.180
to play animation montages for characters.

36
00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:25.549
A skeletal mesh is built up from two parts.

37
00:01:25.549 --> 00:01:27.349
Firstly, we have the set of polygons

38
00:01:27.349 --> 00:01:29.590
which make up the surface of the mesh.

39
00:01:29.590 --> 00:01:31.790
And secondly, we have the hierarchical skeleton

40
00:01:31.790 --> 00:01:34.670
that will be animated to deform those polygons.

41
00:01:34.670 --> 00:01:36.269
A skeletal mesh stores a reference

42
00:01:36.269 --> 00:01:39.030
to the skeleton asset that it was created from.

43
00:01:39.030 --> 00:01:41.629
Skeletal meshes also hold levels of detailed data

44
00:01:41.629 --> 00:01:43.750
that can be used when considering performance costs

45
00:01:43.750 --> 00:01:46.090
of skeletal meshes in your game.

46
00:01:46.090 --> 00:01:47.349
When it comes to gameplay,

47
00:01:47.349 --> 00:01:48.750
this is the mesh type that we can use

48
00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:50.670
for our player characters or indeed anything

49
00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:53.469
that need to animate based off of a skeleton.

50
00:01:53.469 --> 00:01:55.430
A character will hold a skeletal mesh component

51
00:01:55.430 --> 00:01:56.590
which will in turn hold reference

52
00:01:56.590 --> 00:01:58.670
to a skeletal mesh itself.

53
00:01:58.670 --> 00:02:01.549
Skeletal meshes can only play animation sequences

54
00:02:01.549 --> 00:02:03.870
which are tied to its skeleton asset.

55
00:02:03.870 --> 00:02:04.689
This is important to know

56
00:02:04.689 --> 00:02:06.870
when considering playing animation sequences

57
00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:08.620
on the character.

58
00:02:08.620 --> 00:02:10.740
Animation sequences are the building block

59
00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:13.340
for creating an animation blueprint.

60
00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:16.199
An animation sequence is a single animation asset

61
00:02:16.199 --> 00:02:19.129
that can be played on a skeletal mesh.

62
00:02:19.129 --> 00:02:20.689
They contain all the key frames

63
00:02:20.689 --> 00:02:22.810
from our animation authoring package of choice

64
00:02:22.810 --> 00:02:25.050
that specify transform information.

65
00:02:25.050 --> 00:02:27.270
That being your location, rotation and scale

66
00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:29.009
at a specific point in time

67
00:02:29.009 --> 00:02:31.430
and are linked to a specific skeleton.

68
00:02:31.430 --> 00:02:33.030
We can't just stick an animation sequence

69
00:02:33.030 --> 00:02:36.960
on top of any old skeleton, they need to be compatible.

70
00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:38.439
We can think of an animation sequence

71
00:02:38.439 --> 00:02:40.879
as your looping walk animation asset

72
00:02:40.879 --> 00:02:43.689
or your idly kicking the ground asset.

73
00:02:43.689 --> 00:02:46.430
Whilst animation sequences can be played through code,

74
00:02:46.430 --> 00:02:48.229
it's much more typical for them to be used

75
00:02:48.229 --> 00:02:50.689
as building blocks when authoring animation instances

76
00:02:50.689 --> 00:02:53.919
which we'll touch on in just a second.

77
00:02:53.919 --> 00:02:56.620
They also allow developers to apply anim notifies

78
00:02:56.620 --> 00:03:00.509
which again we will touch on in just a second.

79
00:03:00.509 --> 00:03:03.990
Animation sequences also support animation curve data

80
00:03:03.990 --> 00:03:05.550
which provide a way to change the value

81
00:03:05.550 --> 00:03:07.789
of a material parameter or a morph target

82
00:03:07.789 --> 00:03:10.990
while the animation in question is playing back.

83
00:03:10.990 --> 00:03:14.030
Lastly, animation sequences can generate root motion.

84
00:03:14.030 --> 00:03:15.909
However, in regards to root motion,

85
00:03:15.909 --> 00:03:17.610
you need to consider whether you need this

86
00:03:17.610 --> 00:03:19.669
to be replicated in a networked game.

87
00:03:19.669 --> 00:03:21.990
If this is the case, then animation montages

88
00:03:21.990 --> 00:03:24.409
is what you'll need to use instead.

89
00:03:24.409 --> 00:03:28.479
And that leads us nicely onto montages.

90
00:03:28.479 --> 00:03:30.639
What if we were to have multiple animation sequences

91
00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:32.400
that we wanted to play together?

92
00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:35.240
This is where the anim montage comes in.

93
00:03:35.240 --> 00:03:38.159
Anim montages provide a way to control an animation asset

94
00:03:38.159 --> 00:03:40.759
directly through blueprint or C++.

95
00:03:40.759 --> 00:03:42.919
More importantly, an animation montage can be used

96
00:03:42.919 --> 00:03:45.240
to combine several different animation sequences

97
00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:48.030
into a single animation asset.

98
00:03:48.030 --> 00:03:50.349
Furthermore, animation montages can be broken

99
00:03:50.349 --> 00:03:52.110
into sections which can be played

100
00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:55.699
either individually or in combination.

101
00:03:55.699 --> 00:03:58.099
These sections can also be made to loop continuously

102
00:03:58.099 --> 00:04:00.759
until told otherwise.

103
00:04:00.759 --> 00:04:02.719
Events can be fired within a montage

104
00:04:02.719 --> 00:04:05.240
that can perform tasks such as playing a sound key

105
00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:07.949
or changing the ammunition count,

106
00:04:07.949 --> 00:04:12.080
as well as replicating tasks in a networked game.

107
00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:14.740
Complex animation sequences can be assigned to slots

108
00:04:14.740 --> 00:04:16.560
within an animation montage,

109
00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:18.040
the playback of which can be controlled

110
00:04:18.040 --> 00:04:20.720
through blueprint or C++.

111
00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:23.319
Each section created has a name and a location

112
00:04:23.319 --> 00:04:25.279
in the slot, and we can use this name

113
00:04:25.279 --> 00:04:27.959
to either jump directly to a section of the montage

114
00:04:27.959 --> 00:04:29.079
or queue it up to be played

115
00:04:29.079 --> 00:04:32.040
after the current section has been completed.

116
00:04:32.079 --> 00:04:33.879
To put the above information into context,

117
00:04:33.879 --> 00:04:35.819
if we were to take a reload animation,

118
00:04:35.819 --> 00:04:39.319
we can break the reload montage into the following.

119
00:04:39.319 --> 00:04:42.399
Firstly, a start animation sequence,

120
00:04:42.399 --> 00:04:45.480
a reload loop animation sequence right in the middle,

121
00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:48.480
and a finished reloading animation.

122
00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:50.319
We can combine these animation sequences

123
00:04:50.319 --> 00:04:54.110
and assign them to play within sections of the montage.

124
00:04:54.110 --> 00:04:57.689
Our gameplay code can trigger the start reload section,

125
00:04:57.689 --> 00:04:59.629
cause us to stay within the looping section

126
00:04:59.629 --> 00:05:01.629
as we fill the magazine back up,

127
00:05:01.629 --> 00:05:02.829
and then gracefully transition

128
00:05:02.829 --> 00:05:07.300
into the finished animation section when the clip is full.

129
00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:09.439
So we mentioned anim notifies earlier,

130
00:05:09.439 --> 00:05:12.089
so let's take a look at those.

131
00:05:12.089 --> 00:05:14.850
An anim notify is an event fired from the timeline

132
00:05:14.850 --> 00:05:16.389
of an animation sequence,

133
00:05:16.389 --> 00:05:17.930
letting us drive some kind of gameplay

134
00:05:17.930 --> 00:05:21.000
based directly off of an animation.

135
00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:23.699
Let's take a walk cycle as an example.

136
00:05:23.699 --> 00:05:24.899
We can see in this example

137
00:05:24.899 --> 00:05:27.339
that the notifies can be of a particular type.

138
00:05:27.339 --> 00:05:28.779
A great example here would be the use

139
00:05:28.779 --> 00:05:31.980
of the play sound notify to play the sound of a foot fall

140
00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:34.889
as each foot makes contact with the ground.

141
00:05:34.889 --> 00:05:36.649
We could then expand off of this quite easily

142
00:05:36.649 --> 00:05:38.689
and add additional notifies to draw decals

143
00:05:38.689 --> 00:05:41.569
under each foot to draw a footprint.

144
00:05:41.569 --> 00:05:43.370
As can be seen in this example here,

145
00:05:43.370 --> 00:05:44.970
the Unreal Engine offers out of the box

146
00:05:44.970 --> 00:05:46.430
animation notifies that can be used

147
00:05:46.430 --> 00:05:48.089
to achieve many things.

148
00:05:48.089 --> 00:05:50.129
We can also create our own animation notifies

149
00:05:50.129 --> 00:05:53.079
both within native and blueprint.

150
00:05:53.079 --> 00:05:54.399
We can create anim notifies

151
00:05:54.399 --> 00:05:56.879
by simply basing our new anim notify class

152
00:05:56.879 --> 00:05:59.769
from the anim notify state class.

153
00:05:59.769 --> 00:06:01.870
Anim notifies give developers some key functions

154
00:06:01.870 --> 00:06:03.389
that we can override to achieve our goals,

155
00:06:03.910 --> 00:06:06.759
and these are receive notify tick,

156
00:06:06.759 --> 00:06:08.759
receive notify end,

157
00:06:08.759 --> 00:06:13.269
receive notify begin and get notify name.

158
00:06:13.269 --> 00:06:15.629
It isn't necessary to implement every function.

159
00:06:15.629 --> 00:06:17.230
Your use case might just be that you need

160
00:06:17.230 --> 00:06:20.189
to perform something when an anim notify begins,

161
00:06:20.189 --> 00:06:21.269
or you might have a use case

162
00:06:21.269 --> 00:06:22.850
for doing something over a period of time,

163
00:06:22.850 --> 00:06:24.230
in which case you'd most likely use

164
00:06:24.230 --> 00:06:27.230
the notify tick function.

165
00:06:27.230 --> 00:06:29.230
Above are just examples of the functions

166
00:06:29.230 --> 00:06:30.310
that we've just discussed.

167
00:06:30.310 --> 00:06:31.329
And at a bare minimum,

168
00:06:31.329 --> 00:06:33.230
you should implement the notify tick

169
00:06:33.269 --> 00:06:37.120
and both begin notify and begin end functions.

170
00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:38.199
It's also important to note

171
00:06:38.199 --> 00:06:40.000
that the incoming prompts of these functions

172
00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:42.079
offer up are the mesh component,

173
00:06:42.079 --> 00:06:43.720
this being the skeletal mesh component

174
00:06:43.720 --> 00:06:46.740
of the animation that we're triggering the notify on,

175
00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:47.720
the animation,

176
00:06:47.720 --> 00:06:49.600
this is the specific animation sequence

177
00:06:49.600 --> 00:06:52.220
or montage that is triggering this notify,

178
00:06:52.220 --> 00:06:53.519
and the frame delta time,

179
00:06:53.519 --> 00:06:54.540
and this will give you the time

180
00:06:54.540 --> 00:06:57.610
since the last animation update.

181
00:06:57.610 --> 00:07:00.870
The animation instance is a C++ implementation

182
00:07:00.870 --> 00:07:03.370
from which animation blueprints are based.

183
00:07:03.370 --> 00:07:04.629
They're more commonly referred to

184
00:07:04.629 --> 00:07:07.660
as just animation blueprints.

185
00:07:07.660 --> 00:07:09.620
Animation instances are visual scripts

186
00:07:09.620 --> 00:07:12.100
that are used to author complex animation systems.

187
00:07:12.100 --> 00:07:15.879
So consider your players animations.

188
00:07:15.879 --> 00:07:19.500
As an example, let's take an FPS character.

189
00:07:19.500 --> 00:07:21.259
We'd want to control not only how they animate

190
00:07:21.259 --> 00:07:22.579
when moving in 3D,

191
00:07:22.579 --> 00:07:24.060
that being their forwards locomotion,

192
00:07:24.060 --> 00:07:25.980
but also how they walk backwards, strafe,

193
00:07:25.980 --> 00:07:28.759
crouch, sprint, jump, and aimed down sights.

194
00:07:28.759 --> 00:07:29.660
We'd also want to control

195
00:07:29.660 --> 00:07:32.339
how these animations blend into one another.

196
00:07:32.339 --> 00:07:35.889
This is the job of the animation instance.

197
00:07:35.889 --> 00:07:38.649
The animation instance consists of two separate systems,

198
00:07:38.649 --> 00:07:41.610
that being the event graph and the anim graph.

199
00:07:41.610 --> 00:07:43.209
These separate systems work together

200
00:07:43.209 --> 00:07:46.660
to create the correct pose each frame.

201
00:07:46.660 --> 00:07:48.899
The event graph inside the animation instance

202
00:07:48.899 --> 00:07:51.259
is the same as any other graph inside Unreal.

203
00:07:51.259 --> 00:07:53.959
It's a visually scripted environment to drive gameplay

204
00:07:53.959 --> 00:07:57.709
through a context specific set of nodes.

205
00:07:57.709 --> 00:07:58.550
In this case,

206
00:07:58.550 --> 00:08:00.550
we have access to specialized animation nodes,

207
00:08:00.550 --> 00:08:02.910
such as blueprint initialize animation

208
00:08:02.910 --> 00:08:05.939
and blueprint update animation.

209
00:08:05.980 --> 00:08:08.060
Initialize essentially being the begin play

210
00:08:08.060 --> 00:08:10.959
of our animation with the update being its tick,

211
00:08:10.959 --> 00:08:14.509
with the delta sign being passed in accordingly.

212
00:08:14.509 --> 00:08:16.470
The event graph is where we receive data

213
00:08:16.470 --> 00:08:18.029
that will inform the anim graph

214
00:08:18.029 --> 00:08:20.569
as to what pose to produce.

215
00:08:20.569 --> 00:08:24.250
For instance, we may have set an isCrouchingBool to true.

216
00:08:24.250 --> 00:08:26.490
We'll want the event graph to receive that change

217
00:08:26.490 --> 00:08:28.509
and the anim graph to reflect that change

218
00:08:28.509 --> 00:08:30.910
in its subsequent animations.

219
00:08:30.910 --> 00:08:33.409
It's also important to note that in the examples above,

220
00:08:33.409 --> 00:08:34.950
both of these blueprint events are fired

221
00:08:34.990 --> 00:08:38.210
prior to the animation graph having been updated.

222
00:08:38.210 --> 00:08:40.049
If you find that you need more of an update

223
00:08:40.049 --> 00:08:41.789
post the animation graph update,

224
00:08:41.789 --> 00:08:43.830
then consider using the blueprint post

225
00:08:43.830 --> 00:08:47.279
evaluate function instead.

226
00:08:47.279 --> 00:08:51.600
The anim graph is where the actual pose is evaluated.

227
00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:53.779
We can see here that we've got two potential poses

228
00:08:53.779 --> 00:08:55.360
coming in on the left.

229
00:08:55.360 --> 00:08:59.529
One being that of running and the other being crouching.

230
00:08:59.529 --> 00:09:01.769
The anim graph is taking an alpha between the two

231
00:09:01.769 --> 00:09:05.279
and producing a single result with the blend node.

232
00:09:05.279 --> 00:09:07.799
After that blend node, we have an aim offset applied

233
00:09:07.799 --> 00:09:09.559
because after all in an FPS game,

234
00:09:09.559 --> 00:09:11.440
the player hasn't suddenly stopped aiming

235
00:09:11.440 --> 00:09:13.639
now that they're crouching.

236
00:09:13.639 --> 00:09:15.360
The anim graph takes a base pose

237
00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:16.960
and modifies parts of it to reflect

238
00:09:16.960 --> 00:09:19.350
any additional gameplay data.

239
00:09:19.350 --> 00:09:22.570
The anim graph comes with threading out of the box.

240
00:09:22.570 --> 00:09:24.649
There's obviously a lot of stuff going on here.

241
00:09:24.649 --> 00:09:27.529
So think of all the transformations going on under the hood.

242
00:09:27.529 --> 00:09:28.870
Threading makes a lot of sense

243
00:09:28.870 --> 00:09:32.149
in regards to transform manipulation.

244
00:09:32.149 --> 00:09:34.330
We can enable the multi-threading for anim graphs

245
00:09:34.330 --> 00:09:38.049
by enabling the use multi-threaded animation update setting

246
00:09:38.049 --> 00:09:41.470
in the animation instances class defaults.

247
00:09:41.470 --> 00:09:42.649
Of course, with any threading,

248
00:09:42.649 --> 00:09:44.590
we need to take into account its safety.

249
00:09:44.590 --> 00:09:47.470
And there's a few things that are broadly safe to thread.

250
00:09:47.470 --> 00:09:50.190
Those things would be the anim graph nodes themselves,

251
00:09:50.190 --> 00:09:52.940
such as the ones we can see on this slide.

252
00:09:52.940 --> 00:09:55.580
We've also got directly accessing any member variables

253
00:09:55.580 --> 00:09:57.899
such as the is crouching member.

254
00:09:57.899 --> 00:10:00.019
And then finally, any blueprint pure functions

255
00:10:00.019 --> 00:10:02.440
as their cons, they're not going to modify the state

256
00:10:02.440 --> 00:10:05.100
and are thus safe to access on other threads.

257
00:10:06.559 --> 00:10:10.080
We also have the ability to enable fast path optimizations,

258
00:10:10.080 --> 00:10:12.509
which are enabled in the project settings.

259
00:10:12.509 --> 00:10:14.110
This essentially enables the anim graph

260
00:10:14.110 --> 00:10:16.110
to access internal variables directly

261
00:10:16.110 --> 00:10:18.350
without having to copy them from the native

262
00:10:18.350 --> 00:10:20.809
into the blueprint virtual machine.

263
00:10:20.809 --> 00:10:22.570
Of course, this does come with caveats.

264
00:10:22.570 --> 00:10:24.450
If we're intending to modify that value,

265
00:10:24.450 --> 00:10:26.610
we'll lose out on that optimization.

266
00:10:26.610 --> 00:10:27.850
And as such, it's useful to have

267
00:10:27.850 --> 00:10:29.649
the native animation instance class

268
00:10:29.649 --> 00:10:31.230
make any changes that you would want

269
00:10:31.230 --> 00:10:35.370
before having it bubble up into the animation graphs VM.

270
00:10:35.409 --> 00:10:37.370
State machines and animation layers

271
00:10:37.370 --> 00:10:38.450
can be used to organize

272
00:10:38.450 --> 00:10:41.250
and break up animation functionality.

273
00:10:41.250 --> 00:10:43.570
We can see here that we've got separate nodes for idle

274
00:10:43.570 --> 00:10:47.120
and for running with a transition link between the two.

275
00:10:47.120 --> 00:10:49.039
This lets us set up a spoke node layout

276
00:10:49.039 --> 00:10:52.360
for each state in isolation from one another.

277
00:10:52.360 --> 00:10:54.700
These links can have conditions applied to each other,

278
00:10:54.700 --> 00:10:57.500
preventing certain states from blending into each other.

279
00:10:57.500 --> 00:10:58.679
So in the above example,

280
00:10:58.679 --> 00:11:01.279
the run state can blend into the jump start state,

281
00:11:01.279 --> 00:11:04.159
but jump start can never blend back into run.
